22
00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:23.589
Justin Florentine: I think the first thing that we want to cover is some updates to the release process.

23
00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:41.629
Justin Florentine: We haven't quite made them public yet, but we are working on them, and they will. I think what folks need to have as a takeaway here is an understanding that we are going to be increasing our release cadence. So expect releases to come out more frequently.

24
00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:57.939
Justin Florentine: This is necessary due to the large volume of external contributions that we're getting, the quality of which has gone up, which is good. I know we've talked about this in the past, and we were getting a lot of trash. We still get a little bit of trash.

25
00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:04.399
Justin Florentine: But less. So the signal-to-noise ratio has improved on our contributions,

26
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:20.629
Justin Florentine: from AI. We also are getting a number of security-related issues. You may notice in the most recent release, 26.7.1 release, there were, quite a few security… well, there was a vague header that there are security issues in here.

27
00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:45.469
Justin Florentine: And, we will be releasing via GHSA, the GitHub Security Advisory System, more details on the specifics behind each of those. And we're gonna roll that out probably in another week and a half. We're aiming for next Friday. We want to give our operators plenty of time to adopt the new release, start running it, get it deployed, etc.

28
00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.480
Justin Florentine: Before we release details on, on the issues.

29
00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:52.020
Justin Florentine: So, you know…

30
00:05:52.540 --> 00:06:08.459
Justin Florentine: be aware that that's a process. If you do find a security issue, please open a GHSA. There's also a pull request outstanding right now where we're going to make some updates to our security policy that describes all of this and goes into more detail.

31
00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:20.880
Justin Florentine: So, these things are in flux, we're iterating on them very quickly internally, and, we'll make sure that this all gets published.

32
00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:34.089
Justin Florentine: Soon. For most of our folks, this doesn't really matter, because you're not executing releases, this is more of a matter for our release engineers, but we did want to make sure that folks should expect a faster cadence.

33
00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:42.449
Justin Florentine: And perhaps that will mean there's more frequent messages in Discord saying, like, hey, we're cutting a release, you know, do you have anything that has to get in, etc.

