00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:52.330
Justin Florentine: So, one of the things that we have been doing, this kind of is a little bit, related to the release cadence, is we use Calver for our versioning scheme. And,

51
00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:59.319
Justin Florentine: I just wanted to sort of explain why we use that, and how we use that, and…

52
00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:07.069
Justin Florentine: As we get more institutional adoption, where that could maybe be a little bit problematic,

53
00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:11.489
Justin Florentine: We don't plan on changing it. We find that it is a…

54
00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:20.320
Justin Florentine: Good balance of complexity and, precision, and identifying our releases.

55
00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:36.800
Justin Florentine: Where it fails, and we acknowledge this, is that there is no real ready way to apply certain fixes or patch releases. We don't do patch releases. It's just too much work to maintain.

56
00:09:37.080 --> 00:10:00.580
Justin Florentine: And so this means that if we get into situations where a user wants to continue to run, let's say, 26.6, and we release 2671, like we just did, and 26.71 contains a bunch of security patches, they don't really have an option to go and continue to run their older version, but adopt all the security patches.

57
00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:14.669
Justin Florentine: I can appreciate how that may be challenging for institutional folks, however, we simply don't have the bandwidth to maintain older releases and patch them.

58
00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:23.539
Justin Florentine: So I just kind of wanted to get that out there. You know, there has been some discussions as, you know, could we do something along those lines? We don't, but…

59
00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:28.580
Justin Florentine: when we, publish everything via GHSA,

60
00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:42.699
Justin Florentine: all of the fixes for those security issues will be tied back to individual commits. And, there will be a branch, I think, that represents the collection of those.

61
00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:53.119
Justin Florentine: So, we're going to encourage users that need a patch on an older release to kind of roll their own. There also is some pretty good

62
00:10:53.480 --> 00:11:05.930
Justin Florentine: self-documenting code in our GitHub workflows on how to run acceptance tests, integration tests, etc. Even if you just create a fork of Besu, you'll get all that stuff for free. You'll have to do a little bit of configuration on it.

63
00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:18.429
Justin Florentine: But, you know, that should at least allow you to vet to some degree that when you roll your own release, you can apply some of the, you know, CICD oversight that we have normally.

64
00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:28.359
Justin Florentine: So that's where we stand. Calvar, we don't imagine any changes. It's still going to be month, year-month index.

